@page
@model BeeMemoryBank.Web.Pages.Admin.ConnectDeviceModel

@section About {
    <p>
        Use this to join a phone or another computer to this node over your local network, without changing any permanent setting. Press <strong>Connect a device</strong> below:
        this node opens to the network for <strong>15 minutes</strong> (or until one device has joined) and shows a <strong>join code</strong> and a QR code. The code carries the address of this node and a
        one-time token, and it also proves to the other device that it reached this computer and not an impostor on the network. When the 15 minutes are over,
        nothing stays open. Opening this page opens nothing; only the button does.
    </p>
    <p>
        On a computer, paste the code under <strong>Connect to another device</strong> on its first-run page. On a phone, scan the QR code or paste the code in the app.
        The device that joins becomes a trusted node of this network (see <a href="/Admin/TrustedNodes">Trusted Nodes</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
        If you would rather keep this node reachable all the time, switch on <a href="/Admin/DevicesOnMyNetwork">Devices on my network</a> instead.
    </p>
}

<div class="card mt-md" id="connect-device">
    @if (Model.Lan.Error != null)
    {
        <sl-alert variant="danger" open style="margin-bottom:16px;">
            <sl-icon slot="icon" name="exclamation-octagon"></sl-icon>
            @Model.Lan.Error
        </sl-alert>
    }

    @if (Model.Lan.Mode == "unavailable")
    {
        <sl-alert variant="warning" open style="margin-bottom:16px;">
            <sl-icon slot="icon" name="exclamation-triangle"></sl-icon>
            <strong>This node cannot open a LAN listener.</strong> Connecting a device this way
            works in the BeeMemoryBank desktop apps (Windows and Mac) and in the Windows service.
            With Docker, the published ports in <code>docker-compose.yml</code> decide what other computers can reach
            (see <code>docs/deployment.md</code>). Other devices can join through a hub.
        </sl-alert>
    }
    else
    {
        @if (Model.Lan.FirewallManaged && !Model.Lan.FirewallRule)
        {
            <div id="connect-firewall" style="border:1px solid var(--sl-color-warning-600);border-radius:10px;padding:16px;margin-bottom:16px;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.5;">
                <p style="margin:0 0 8px;"><strong>Windows Firewall will block the phone.</strong> To let devices on your
                   local network reach this computer on port @Model.Lan.Port, BeeMemoryBank needs
                   to add one firewall rule. Windows will ask for administrator permission; this
                   happens once, and BeeMemoryBank never changes the firewall without asking.</p>
                <form data-busy="short" method="post" asp-page-handler="Firewall">
                    <sl-button type="submit" variant="default">
                        <sl-icon slot="prefix" name="shield-check"></sl-icon>
                        Allow in Windows Firewall&hellip;
                    </sl-button>
                </form>
            </div>
        }

        @if (Model.Lan.Platform == "macos")
        {
            <div style="border:1px solid var(--sl-color-warning-600);border-radius:10px;padding:16px;margin-bottom:16px;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.5;">
                <p style="margin:0;"><strong>On a Mac, macOS asks for permission.</strong> When the first device connects, macOS asks whether
                   &ldquo;Bee Memory Bank&rdquo; may accept incoming network connections. Choose <strong>Allow</strong>.
                   Bee Memory Bank does not change the firewall itself.</p>
            </div>
        }
        else if (!Model.Lan.FirewallManaged && Model.Lan.Platform == "windows")
        {
            <div style="border:1px solid var(--sl-color-warning-600);border-radius:10px;padding:16px;margin-bottom:16px;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.5;">
                <p style="margin:0;"><strong>Windows service.</strong> The firewall is not changed from here: the installer's
                   &ldquo;Configure Windows Firewall Exception&rdquo; option opens port @Model.Lan.Port. If you did not
                   choose it, add an inbound rule for TCP @Model.Lan.Port yourself.</p>
            </div>
        }

        @if (!Model.Lan.Active && Model.Lan.Setting == "on")
        {
            <sl-alert variant="danger" open style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <sl-icon slot="icon" name="exclamation-octagon"></sl-icon>
                <strong>Devices on my network is on, but the node could not open port @Model.Lan.Port.</strong>
                Another program may be using it. See <a href="/Admin/DevicesOnMyNetwork">Devices on my network</a>.
            </sl-alert>
        }
        else if (!Model.Lan.Active)
        {
            <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin:0 0 12px;">This computer is not listening on the network right now.
               Opening it lets one device join in the next 15 minutes; it closes by itself once
               the device has joined or the time is up. The vault stays unlocked.</p>
            <form data-busy="short" method="post" asp-page-handler="Enable">
                <sl-button type="submit" variant="primary" size="small">
                    <sl-icon slot="prefix" name="phone"></sl-icon>
                    Connect a device
                </sl-button>
            </form>
        }
        else if (Model.HasLanAddress)
        {
            @if (Model.Lan.ExpiresAt is { } expiresAt)
            {
                <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin:0 0 12px;">Open until @expiresAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm"), or until a device has joined.</p>
            }
            else if (Model.Lan.Mode == "permanent")
            {
                <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin:0 0 12px;">These codes do not expire: Devices on my network is on (<a href="/Admin/DevicesOnMyNetwork">Devices on my network</a>).</p>
            }
            @foreach (var ep in Model.Endpoints)
            {
                <div class="connect-endpoint" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:12px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:20px;margin-bottom:16px;background:var(--bg-app);">
                    <img src="@ep.QrDataUri" alt="Join code for @ep.Url" style="width:256px;height:256px;image-rendering:pixelated;" />
                    <span style="font-family:monospace;font-size:0.95rem;word-break:break-all;text-align:center;color:var(--sl-color-primary-400);">@ep.Url</span>
                    <sl-copy-button value="@ep.Code" copy-label="Copy join code"></sl-copy-button>
                    <code style="font-size:0.72rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere;color:var(--text-secondary);">@ep.Code</code>
                </div>
            }
            <div class="admin-notes text-muted text-sm" style="line-height:1.5;">
                <p>On a phone, choose <strong>Join</strong>, paste this join code (or scan the
                   QR with the camera and copy its text), and enter the master password. The
                   phone checks this computer's key from the code before it sends the password
                   anywhere.</p>
                <p>On another computer running Bee Memory Bank, open its first-run page, choose
                   <strong>Connect to another device</strong>, paste this join code and enter the master
                   password. It checks this computer's key the same way. With <code>bmb join</code>, use
                   <code>--code</code>.</p>
            </div>
            @if (Model.Lan.Mode == "on-demand")
            {
                <p class="admin-note text-muted text-sm">This door is for joining only, and closes afterwards. For another computer to keep
                   syncing with this one it has to reach this computer again: switch on
                   <strong>Devices on my network</strong> under <a href="/Admin/DevicesOnMyNetwork">Devices on my network</a>.</p>
                <form data-busy="short" method="post" asp-page-handler="Disable" style="margin-top:12px;">
                    <sl-button type="submit" variant="default" size="small">Stop listening</sl-button>
                </form>
            }
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="text-muted" style="text-align:center;padding:24px 0;">No LAN IPv4 address was detected on this machine. Connect to a network and refresh.</p>
        }
    }
</div>

<div class="card mt-md" id="connect-certificate">
    <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;"><sl-icon name="download"></sl-icon> Open this node's web pages from another device</h3>
    <div class="admin-notes text-muted text-sm" style="line-height:1.5;">
        <p>Joining with the code above needs no certificate. To open this node's web pages from
           another device's browser, that device must trust this node's CA certificate: open
           the downloaded <code>ca.crt</code> on it and install it.</p>
    </div>
    <sl-button variant="primary" size="small" href="/connect/ca.crt" download>
        <sl-icon slot="prefix" name="download"></sl-icon>
        Download CA certificate (ca.crt)
    </sl-button>

    <div class="admin-notes text-muted text-sm" style="line-height:1.5;">
        <h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;color:var(--text-primary);">Android</h4>
        <p>Open the file, name the certificate (e.g. <code>BeeMemoryBank</code>), and choose
           <strong>VPN and apps</strong> as the use. Then in
           <em>Settings &rarr; Security &rarr; Encryption &amp; credentials &rarr; Trusted
           credentials</em>, confirm it appears under the user tab.</p>

        <h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;color:var(--text-primary);">iOS</h4>
        <p>Open the file to add a profile under
           <em>Settings &rarr; Profile Downloaded &rarr; Install</em>. <strong>This is not
           enough on iOS</strong> &mdash; afterwards go to
           <em>Settings &rarr; General &rarr; About &rarr; Certificate Trust Settings</em> and
           enable full trust for this CA. Until that switch is on, iOS still rejects the
           connection.</p>

        <h4 style="margin:18px 0 6px;color:var(--text-primary);">Windows / macOS</h4>
        <p>Double-click <code>ca.crt</code> and place it in the <strong>Trusted Root
           Certification Authorities</strong> (Windows) or <strong>System</strong> keychain
           (macOS, mark it as <em>Always Trust</em>).</p>
    </div>
</div>
